## MVP

The MVP must support:

```bash
npx devpreview
npx devpreview --port 3000
npx devpreview --help
npx devpreview --version
```

Core functionality:

* Detect local development server.
* Accept explicit port.
* Start a tunnel automatically.
* Generate/use a temporary public HTTPS URL.
* Print the URL clearly.
* Display tunnel status.
* Gracefully stop the tunnel with `Ctrl+C`.
* Clean up child processes.
* Handle tunnel failures clearly.

---

## Current Tunnel Strategy

Use **Cloudflare Quick Tunnel** as the initial tunnel implementation.

Conceptually:

```text
DevPreview CLI
      │
      ▼
cloudflared
      │
      ▼
localhost:<port>
```

The CLI should manage `cloudflared` automatically.

Do not require users to manually configure:

* DNS
* domains
* certificates
* Cloudflare accounts
* API keys

---

## Important Architecture Rule

Cloudflare must NOT be deeply coupled to the rest of the application.

Create a tunnel abstraction:

```ts
interface TunnelProvider {
  start(port: number): Promise<TunnelSession>;
  stop(): Promise<void>;
}
```

Initial implementation:

```text
CloudflareTunnelProvider
```

Future providers can include:

```text
ZrokTunnelProvider
BoreTunnelProvider
FrpTunnelProvider
```

---

## Do Not Build Yet

Do NOT implement these unless explicitly requested:

* Custom domains
* User accounts
* Billing
* Team management
* Cloud-hosted databases
* Full deployment infrastructure
* Kubernetes
* Complex analytics
* AI features
* GitHub integration
* PR previews
* Persistent preview environments
* Cloud IDE
* Full observability platform

The MVP is a tunnel + excellent CLI experience.

---

## Development Philosophy

Prefer the simplest implementation that works.

Before adding a dependency:

1. Check whether Node.js provides the functionality.
2. Check whether an existing dependency already solves it.
3. Only then add a new dependency.

Avoid abstractions that do not have an immediate use.

Do not create large frameworks around simple functionality.

---

## Code Quality

Prefer:

* TypeScript
* Small modules
* Explicit types
* Async/await
* Clear error handling
* Platform-aware process handling
* Testable functions
* Minimal side effects

Avoid:

* `any`
* unnecessary classes
* global mutable state
* deeply nested callbacks
* giant files
* premature abstractions

---

## CLI UX

The CLI is the product.

Output should be:

```text
DevPreview

✓ Local server found
✓ Starting tunnel
✓ Preview ready

  https://example.trycloudflare.com

  Local:  http://localhost:3000
  Status: Connected

Press Ctrl+C to stop
```

Errors should be actionable.

Bad:

```text
Error: spawn ENOENT
```

Good:

```text
✗ Could not start cloudflared

cloudflared was not found.

Install cloudflared and run DevPreview again.
```

Never dump raw stack traces for normal user-facing failures.

---

## Process Management

The CLI will launch an external tunnel process.

It must:

* capture stdout/stderr
* detect successful tunnel creation
* extract the public URL
* forward relevant errors
* terminate the child process on exit
* handle SIGINT
* handle SIGTERM
* avoid orphaned processes

Test process cleanup explicitly.

---

## Cross Platform

The CLI should eventually support:

* macOS
* Linux
* Windows

Do not assume:

```text
/bin/bash
```

or Unix-only commands.

Use Node.js APIs for process management and filesystem operations whenever possible.

---

## Testing

Every core behavior should have tests.

Minimum coverage:

```text
Port parsing
Port validation
Port detection
CLI arguments
Tunnel URL extraction
Tunnel startup failure
Tunnel shutdown
SIGINT handling
Invalid port
Port unavailable
```

Tests must not depend on a real public tunnel unless explicitly marked as integration tests.

Mock external tunnel processes in unit tests.

---

## Git Rules

Keep commits small and focused.

Prefer:

```text
feat: add tunnel provider interface
feat: add cloudflare tunnel provider
feat: add automatic port detection
fix: cleanup tunnel on SIGINT
test: add tunnel URL parsing tests
```

Avoid:

```text
update stuff
changes
final
fixed
```

Never commit:

```text
.env
API keys
tokens
credentials
local configuration
```

---

## Agent Workflow

Before changing code:

1. Inspect the existing project.
2. Understand the current architecture.
3. Find the smallest relevant change.
4. Implement it.
5. Run tests.
6. Run typecheck/lint.
7. Verify CLI behavior.
8. Report exactly what changed.

Do not rewrite unrelated code.

Do not refactor unrelated files.

Do not introduce new architecture unless necessary.

---

## Definition of Done

A feature is complete only when:

* It works locally.
* Relevant tests pass.
* TypeScript passes.
* Lint passes.
* Errors are handled.
* Processes are cleaned up.
* No secrets are exposed.
* CLI output is understandable.
* Documentation is updated when user-facing behavior changes.

---

## Golden Rule

**Do not build a complicated platform when a developer only asked for a shareable localhost URL.**
